<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Your .com is taken. Now what? | Domain Hound</title>
  <meta name="description" content="{{description}}">
  <meta property="og:type" content="article">
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="Domain Hound">
  <meta property="og:title" content="Your .com is taken. Now what?">
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{description}}">
  <meta property="og:image" content="https://domainhound.dev/sf/share.png">
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{description}}">
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://domainhound.dev/sf/">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
  <link rel="canonical" href="https://domainhound.dev/sf/">
  <link rel="icon" href="/assets/hound-mark.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/home.css">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/sf/page.css">
  <script type="module" src="/sf/page.mjs"></script>
  <script type="module" src="/sf/funding-table.mjs"></script>
</head>
<body>
<header><div class="frame mast">
  <a class="paper-art" href="/" aria-label="Domain Hound home">
    <span class="paper-word first">Domain</span><span class="paper-word second">Hound</span>
    <span class="paper-dog"><img src="/assets/hound-mark.svg" width="180" height="180" alt=""></span>
  </a>
  <a class="jump" href="#check">Check a name</a>
</div></header>
<main>
  <section class="intro"><div class="frame spread">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">San Francisco Bay Area &nbsp;/&nbsp; {{years}}</p>
    <h1>Your .com is taken.<br>Now what?</h1>
    <p class="lede">See what {{total}} Y Combinator startups picked.</p>
    <div id="check">{{heroCheck}}</div>
    <a class="funding-jump" href="#funding">See funding by company</a></div>
    <div class="ticket"><div class="ticket-top"><span>Website ending</span><b>.ai</b></div>
      <strong>{{ai}}<small>%</small></strong><p>of these startups use <b>.ai</b></p>
      <div class="ticket-foot">{{aiCount}} of {{total}} sites <span>{{month}}</span></div>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="chart-band"><div class="frame">
    <h2>What did startups pick?</h2>
    <p class="deck">Each bar is one YC batch. Pick one to see its endings.</p>
    <div class="legend"><span><i class="com"></i>.com</span><span><i class="ai"></i>.ai</span><span><i class="rest"></i>Other endings</span></div>
    <div class="plot">
      <div class="axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>100%</span><span>50%</span><span>0%</span></div>
      <div class="years">{{bars}}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="batch-readout"><select aria-label="YC batch" id="batch-select" disabled>{{options}}</select><span id="batch-summary" role="status">{{summary}}</span></div>
    <details class="batch-details"><summary>All endings in this batch</summary>
      <table><caption>{{latest}}</caption><thead><tr><th>Ending</th><th>Sites</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{tableRows}}</tbody></table>
    </details>
    <p class="chart-note">.com was the most common ending overall. Just {{siCount}} of {{total}} sites used .si, shown under “Other endings”.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="facts-band pick-band"><div class="frame">
    <h2>Which ending should you pick?</h2>
    <p class="deck">We priced the same {{sampleSize}} names with each ending. These shops had the lowest bill over three years.</p>
    <div class="cards pick-cards">{{picks}}</div>
    <p class="price-date">USD before tax · Domain Hound · {{priceDay}}</p>
    <p class="pick-rule"><strong>Start with your exact .com.</strong> If it’s taken, pick .ai for an AI business or .si to keep the name for less. Want the .com? Try adding a word.</p>
    <p class="chart-note">To buy a taken .com, you deal with its owner. Prices vary.</p>
    <details><summary>Where the prices come from</summary>
      <p>Domain Hound checked {{sampleSize}} available names per ending on {{priceDay}}. All {{sampleSize}} names got the same list price at each shop. We compared the payments due over three years, including renewals.</p>
      <p>{{priceSources}}</p>
    </details>
  </div></section>
  <div class="chart-band trend-band"><div class="frame">
    <section class="ai-trend" aria-labelledby="ai-trend-title">
      <h2 id="ai-trend-title">Has .ai peaked?</h2>
      <p class="chart-note">In {{firstBatch}}, {{firstAi}}% used .ai. The high so far is <strong>{{trendPeak}}</strong>. {{latest}} came in at {{trendLatest}}.</p>
      <figure aria-label="Share of sites using .ai by YC batch">
        <div class="trend-head" aria-hidden="true"><span>YC batch</span><span>.ai share</span><span>Batch size</span></div>
        <div class="trend-plot">{{trend}}</div>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </div></div>
  <section id="funding" class="funding-band"><div class="frame">
    <p class="eyebrow">Funding by company</p>
    <h2>{{fundingHeadline}}</h2>
    <p class="deck">Here are all {{total}} startups and their domains. We matched {{matched}} to SEC filings and ranked those by the amount each reported raising.</p>
    <details class="funding-note"><summary>What the numbers mean</summary>
      <p>“Raised (Form D)” adds up what each company told the SEC it sold through {{day}}. We use the latest filing for each offering. Some rounds never appear here. These are funding totals. They don’t tell us what a company is worth.</p>
      <p>“No match” means we couldn’t confirm a filing under the exact legal name. For “Has the .com?” we drop spaces and punctuation from the brand. “Yes” means YC lists that .com or we saw a redirect between it and the listed site. Otherwise, ownership is unverified.</p>
      <a href="/data/sf/companies.json" download>Download companies and filing sources (JSON)</a>
    </details>
    <div class="funding-tools">
      <label class="funding-picker" hidden>Sort by <select id="funding-sort"></select></label>
      <p id="funding-count" role="status">{{visible}} of {{total}} companies</p>
      <button id="funding-more" aria-controls="company-table" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Show all</button>
    </div>
    <table id="company-table" aria-label="Bay Area YC companies and Form D funding">
      <thead><tr>{{fundingHeaders}}</tr></thead><tbody>{{companyRows}}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div></section>
  <section class="facts-band"><div class="frame">
    <h2>Behind the dot</h2>
    <div class="cards">
      <article><h3>How long is a name?</h3><strong>{{median}}</strong><p>characters before the dot. That’s the median. Names ranged from {{shortest}} to {{longest}} characters.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Get, try or hq?</h3><strong>{{prefixCount}}</strong><p>sites put “get” or “try” before the brand name. “Get” appeared {{get}} times, “try” {{try}} and “hq” {{hq}}.</p></article>
      <article><h3>The .com detour</h3><strong>{{different}}</strong><p>of {{comCount}} .com sites had something other than the exact brand name before the dot.</p></article>
    </div>
    <details><summary>More naming facts and name shapes</summary>
      <p>We found {{distinct}} endings. {{single}} showed up once. Before the dot, {{hyphen}} names had a hyphen and {{digits}} had a digit.</p>
      <h3>How the brand names read</h3>
      <p>We sorted names by spelling. An English dictionary can miss surnames and words in other languages.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Name shape</th><th>Brands</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead><tbody>{{shapes}}</tbody></table>
    </details>
  </div></section>
  <!-- Future input is weekly ending/count aggregates only, with at least 20 per published bucket. -->
  <section hidden id="weekly"><h2>Endings founders searched this week</h2></section>
  <section class="method"><div class="frame">
    <h2>How we counted</h2>
    <p>On {{day}}, we counted one listed site per startup across {{batches}} YC batches, from {{firstBatch}} through {{latest}}. Of {{eligible}} eligible companies, {{excluded}} had no usable site. A startup can change its domain after launch.</p>
    <details><summary>Coverage, sources and terms</summary>
      <p>We included public YC startups of any status with a listed location in a Bay Area town or city, or at Stanford. Each counted once. We left out fully remote companies and future batches.</p>
      <p>We took endings from the <a href="https://publicsuffix.org/list/">Public Suffix List</a> so endings like .co.uk stay together. We measured the name just before the ending. To match brands and prefixes, we kept letters and digits, ignored case and dropped spaces and punctuation. We kept legal suffixes such as Inc.</p>
      <p>For name shapes, <a href="https://www.nltk.org/howto/wordnet.html">WordNet</a> supplies English words. We split compounds at spaces, hyphens or capital letters, or into two words of at least three letters each. A single string of letters with no match counts as “Possibly invented”. Other spellings go under “Other”.</p>
      <p>The sites come from <a href="https://github.com/yc-oss/api">YC OSS</a>, an unofficial feed of the <a href="https://www.ycombinator.com/companies">YC directory</a>. YC’s <a href="https://www.ycombinator.com/legal#tou">terms of use</a> restrict scraping and commercial reuse. The feed grants no separate data license. Domain Hound is independent of YC.</p>
    </details>
    <div class="foot-links"><a href="/data/sf/endings.json" download>Download the counts (JSON)</a><button id="share" hidden>Share this page</button><span id="share-status" role="status"></span></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="check-band"><div class="frame">
    <h2>Which ending can you get?</h2>
    {{closingCheck}}
  </div></section>
</main>
<footer><div class="frame"><a href="/">Domain Hound</a><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a></div></footer>
</body>
</html>
